=== Table K1 - kernel latency, memory-to-memory dependent chain ===
  (median cycles per operation; every backend pays the memory round-trip)

  backend                                  fe_mul    fe_sq
  -------------------------------------- -------- --------
  microcode (this work), full patch         103.8     72.9
  fiat-crypto                               146.1    112.3
  CryptOpt                                  141.3    119.8
  amd64-51 asm (Bernstein-Schwabe)          146.0    120.8
  hand-written __uint128_t C                140.5    104.9
  OpenSSL fe51 asm                           97.1     72.4
  -- saturated 4x64 (other representation)                  
  s2n-bignum (verified asm, 4x64)           108.9    107.4

=== Table K2 - where the microcode cycles go ===
  (median cycles per operation, dependent chain)

  layer                                    fe_mul    fe_sq
  -------------------------------------- -------- --------
  wrapper instr. cost (issue-bound)          10.1      7.2
  wrapper + redirection (1-triad patch)      15.1     15.6
  wrapper + redirection + patch body        103.8     72.9

  derived:
  patch body (full - floor)                  88.6     57.3
  wrapper+redirection, share of total       14.6%    21.4%

  The wrapper row is issue-bound throughput, measured on disjoint
  slots; in situ it overlaps the firing, so it is context for the
  floor rather than a term to subtract from it. The floor IS the
  wrapper+redirection cost a caller cannot avoid (15%/21% of the op).

  drift check: fe_mul re-measured after restore = 103.8 (first pass 103.8, +0.02%)

=== Table K6 - why a firing costs more inside the ladder ===
  (median cycles per operation; each arm is a dependent chain of
   16 ops, same shape as Table K1, differing only as noted)

  arm                                       measured  expected    excess
  ---------------------------------------- --------- --------- ---------
  microcode, alternating mul/sq                 87.9      88.3      -0.5
  OpenSSL, alternating (control)                85.6      84.8      +0.8
  fiat-crypto, alternating (control)           128.2     129.2      -1.0
  microcode, 5 field slots not 2               103.8     103.8      +0.0

  A ladder step issues 9 firings and switches hook 3 times. If the
  alternating excess is microcode-specific (large for the first row,
  ~0 for the controls) the cost is hook/patch-RAM switching. If the
  scatter row carries it instead, it is the working set. Roughly 12
  cycles per firing has to be found here to reconcile Table K1 with
  the measured ladder.

=== Table K5 - arithmetic only, both floors removed ===
  (median cycles per operation, dependent chain, invocation cost
   subtracted from BOTH sides: microcode loses wrapper+redirection,
   the natives lose call + the same 10-load / 5-store round trip)

  microcode floor (wrapper+redirect)         15.1     15.6
  native floor (call + mem round trip)        9.2      6.4

  backend, arithmetic only                 fe_mul    fe_sq
  -------------------------------------- -------- --------
  microcode (this work)                      88.6     57.3
  OpenSSL fe51 asm                           88.0     66.1
  fiat-crypto                               137.0    106.0
  CryptOpt                                  132.2    113.5
  amd64-51 asm                              136.8    114.4
  hand-written C                            131.3     98.5

  microcode arithmetic vs OpenSSL arithmetic: mul 1.008x  sq 0.867x  (<1 = microcode faster)
  CAVEAT: the fe_sq column flatters microcode. FE_SQ's lea/imul
  precompute sits in the microcode floor and is subtracted away,
  while OpenSSL's fe51_sqr keeps its doubling inside the routine.
  fe_mul has no such asymmetry (19*b_j is inside the patch), so the
  fe_mul column is the clean comparison and the one to quote.

